WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.359
We have been given the scientific knowledge
the technical ability and the materials to

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:13.769
pursue the exploration of the universe, to
ignore this great resources would be a corruption

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:19.890
of a god given ability.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:31.660
It’s estimated that about 650 million people
watched the first moon landing in 1969 - nearly

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.460
a quarter of the world’s population.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.710
As the world watched those powerful Saturn
V rocket engines burst into life, they witnessed

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:45.000
plumes of sooty exhaust billow from its 5
shuddering nozzles as it pushed itself off

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.870
the ground, burning through a colossal volume
of kerosene to lift the rocket’s massive

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.690
3 million kilogram weight off the ground.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:59.760
Soon the first stage shut off and separated,
allowing the second stage to roar into life.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.900
This time powered by a different fuel, liquid
hydrogen.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:09.899
A fuel capable of providing better performance,
but it occupied too much space.

00:01:09.899 --> 00:01:14.929
Liquid hydrogen is considerably less dense
than kerosene, so needs to be combined in

00:01:14.929 --> 00:01:18.039
a much higher ratio with liquid oxygen.

00:01:18.039 --> 00:01:23.409
For every litre of liquid oxygen burned in
the Saturn V first stage, it required 0.64

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:29.759
litres of kerosene, while its second stage
needed 3.25 litres of liquid hydrogen for

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:32.020
every litre of oxygen.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:37.520
NASA engineers couldn’t feasibly make the
Saturn V first stage fuel tanks any larger,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.049
so liquid hydrogen was not an option.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:45.740
The science of rocket fuel is a fascinating
and complicated field.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.840
Combining not just physics, chemistry, and
engineering, but also logistics.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.569
That’s the challenge facing Space X as it
develops the next generation of heavy lift

00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:01.860
rockets, designed to take us not just to the
Moon, but further, to Mars.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.060
Starship’s Raptor engines will not use kerosene
or liquid hydrogen.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.990
It will use methane.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:13.230
A fuel that was considered frequently over
the past century of rocket fuel research,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:18.890
with a few honourable mentions in John D Clarks’
seminal book, “Ignition!”, but it has

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.340
never seen widespread use.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.879
So, why is SpaceX using it now?

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:30.080
Putting people on the moon in the 1960s was
one of the greatest technological challenges

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.671
we’d ever faced, but getting humans to Mars
is a considerably more difficult task, especially

00:02:36.671 --> 00:02:42.269
when you factor in the enormous challenge
of keeping humans there - of creating and

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:45.880
sustaining a human settlement on the red planet.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.330
How do you reduce the cost of launches?

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:52.790
How do you make the oxygen needed to stay
alive, how do you provide water for growing

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:59.910
food and for drinking, and how do you make
the fuel to power a return trip to Earth?

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.760
Kerosene and Hydrogen are not perfect.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.099
Kerosene is extracted from crude oil via fractional
distillation and is made up of a mixture of

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:13.110
long-chain hydrocarbons, reaching up to around
20 carbons in length.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.219
The longer the hydrocarbon, the harder it
is for it to burn completely in oxygen, as

00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:23.590
they require more oxygen per gram of fuel
to be completely oxidised into carbon dioxide

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:24.730
and water.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:31.609
And so, even in its refined form, kerosene
often burns incompletely, decomposing instead

00:03:31.609 --> 00:03:34.300
into smaller, reactive radicals.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.849
The result is coking - the production of sooty
carbon particulates that we saw in the Saturn

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:41.270
Vs launch.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:46.659
This soot can easily clog up the intricate
mechanism of a rocket engine, which is a problem

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:52.889
for SpaceX and its goal to make its engines
reusable with minimal maintenance.

00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:58.620
Especially on Mars, where the facilities to
fix these issues will not be available.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.420
Liquid hydrogen, of course, doesn’t have
this problem, and it has the advantage of

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.500
burning more efficiently than kerosene.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.170
We can quantify this efficiency with specific
impulse.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:16.680
Specific impulse describes how efficiently
a fuel can convert its mass into thrust.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.630
To understand this let’s first look at total
impulse, which describes the thrust force

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.770
generated over the entire burn period of the
engine.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.650
We can graph this rather easily, by plotting
the thrust the engine is providing in each

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:34.530
second of its flight, that may look something
like this.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:39.880
The total impulse is found by finding the
area under this graph, which gives us the

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.850
total energy the rocket released.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:49.290
This is a useful metric in itself, but specific
impulse is better, because not all propellants

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:50.780
are born equal.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:56.830
Two different fuel and oxidiser combinations
could provide the same total impulse, but

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:02.370
we need to consider the weight of the fuel
and oxidisers themselves, after all, the initial

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:07.770
weight of rockets is always dominated by the
weight of their own fuel.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:13.170
To find the average specific impulse we divide
the total impulse by total propellant weight

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:14.350
the rocket expelled.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.350
[4]

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:19.950
Going by this metric, a liquid hydrogen and
liquid oxygen fuel mixture is by far the best.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.030
(Table from [1])
Hydrogen has a much higher specific impulse

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:29.140
than kerosene [1] - around 390 seconds, against
kerosenes’s 285 seconds.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:34.370
However, as mentioned earlier, Hydrogen is
much less dense than kerosene.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.360
Requiring much larger fuel tanks.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:43.490
Hydrogen also has an exceptionally low boiling
point at minus 252.8 degrees celsius - and

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:49.120
so the tanks need to be heavily insulated
to avoid expansion of the liquid hydrogen,

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.860
but thermodynamic equilibrium is a war of
attrition that will the universe will always

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:01.760
win, and so it also requires boil off valves
to release gaseous hydrogen to prevent an

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:02.860
explosion.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.850
This all adds mass and complexity to the rocket.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:12.830
Hydrogen also degrades and weakens metals
in a process known as hydrogen embrittlement.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:18.550
This is a massive issue for SpaceX’s reusability
design ethos.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:23.300
Combining two parameters, the density and
the specific heat of combustion, we can get

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:27.080
an idea of the difference between these 3
fuels.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:32.720
If we want to release 100 Megajoules of energy
from each of these three fuels, we would need

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:40.090
11.9 liters of hydrogen, 2.2 liters of kerosene,
or 5.5 liters of methane.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.110
Methane is much closer to kerosene than hydrogen.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.860
Allowing fuel tanks to be smaller than liquid
hydrogen fuel tanks, but not small enough

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.500
to offer much performance benefits over kerosene.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:56.170
When the necessary design changes are made
to switch from kerosene to liquid methane,

00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:02.150
like increasing the fuel tank volume, the
increase in specific impulse is all but negated.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.880
This is why Methane hasn’t seen use yet.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:10.820
Methane simply sits in an awkward middle ground
between the two most popular fuels.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:15.430
It provides better performance than kerosene,
but not as good as hydrogen.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:20.030
And it’s easier to store than hydrogen,
but not as easy as kerosene.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:25.590
Its benefits are only now becoming useful
as SpaceX works to unlock the magic of reusable

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.340
rockets.[2].

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:33.360
Methane is a single-carbon hydrocarbon, so
unlike the long-chain molecules found in kerosene,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.050
it produces significantly less soot when burnt,
leading to less damage to the engine over

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.280
time.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.840
Its boiling point is actually higher than
liquid oxygens.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.690
Allowing much of the necessary infrastructure
to liquify and use oxygen to be also used

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.770
for liquid methane.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.860
Important when working with limited infrastructure
on Mars.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:08:00.280
But, most importantly, the real reason methane
has suddenly become very attractive to SpaceX

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:05.480
is that it can be synthesised from the carbon
dioxide rich atmosphere of Mars.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.570
Mars’ atmosphere is almost entirely carbon
dioxide.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:15.940
95% of the Martian atmosphere is CO2, with
the remaining 5% being made up from gases

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.180
like nitrogen, argon and a trace amount of
oxygen.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:24.480
Whilst this carbon dioxide-rich atmosphere
may be a disadvantage when it comes to establishing

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:30.130
a city on Mars, it provides huge advantages
when it comes to creating rocket fuel.

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:35.550
We have come to see carbon dioxide as a waste
gas - something produced as a by-product of

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:41.560
combustion, instead of as a raw material,
but it has the enormous potential to act as

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.190
a feedstock for the production of methane.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:50.500
Over 100 years ago, a chemist called Paul
Sabatier came up with a process of converting

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:56.570
carbon dioxide into methane by passing it
through a catalyst, usually Nickel, with hydrogen

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:59.400
gas at an elevated temperature and pressure.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:04.830
The reaction takes one mole of carbon dioxide
and reacts it with four moles of hydrogen

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:08.450
to produce one mole of methane and two of
water.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:13.710
When combined with our electrolysis process,
this produces one mole of methane to two moles

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:14.710
of oxygen.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:19.250
The ratio of moles - the stoichiometry - is
going to be important soon.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:23.960
But the first question is, how do you get
your chemical reagents on Mars?

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:29.130
Well for this, we need In Situ Resource Utilisation.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:32.360
Getting carbon dioxide is a relatively easy
task.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:39.170
With an atmosphere made up from 95% CO2, extracting
a pure sample of the compound is straightforward

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:45.890
enough, but we do need to get rid of that
other 5% and condense the carbon dioxide.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:51.600
Currently, cryofreezing is the most viable
option, carbon dioxide has the highest freezing

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.660
point of the gases present in Mars’ atmosphere.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:10:00.640
So, in a process that is essentially the opposite
of distillation, we can cool the air to separate

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:06.980
the carbon dioxide, which will freeze into
a solid while the other gases remain as gas.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:10.010
This also naturally compresses the gas.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:15.700
Then, when we need to use it in our sabatier
reactor it can be simply warmed up to create

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.880
a high pressure stream of gaseous CO2. [3].

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.820
However, getting the necessary hydrogen is
much more difficult.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:28.120
The first option is to import it directly
from Earth, but given how much is required,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:32.260
and the difficulty storing it for long periods,
this isn’t a great option.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:38.130
So, long term, we will want to extract it
from resources available on Mars.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:43.310
Water is contained within Martian soil, but,
most significantly, it’s found in the form

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:46.760
of ice in the polar regions of the planet.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:51.170
If we can find an efficient way of mining
the water, we can then convert it into oxygen

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:57.560
and hydrogen using an electric current, and
then the hydrogen can be combined with carbon

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.570
dioxide to produce methane.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:02.320
Remember when we said the molar ratios would
be important.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.850
Here’s why.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:10.410
That 2:1 molar ratio of oxygen to methane,
gives us a mass ratio of 4:1 of oxygen to

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.410
methane.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:18.150
The propellant mixture employed by SpaceX’s
raptor engine uses a 3.4 : 1 ratio, so this

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:23.240
whole process gives us an excess of oxygen,
which can be put towards the life support

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:25.580
systems in your Martian city [3].

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:26.910
A win win.

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:32.580
But extracting large quantities of water from
Mars, either from ice in the polar regions

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:37.529
or from the small quantities of liquid water
in the soil, would not be easy, and the technology

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:41.430
to do this on a large scale doesn’t exist
yet.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:46.600
Over the short term, if we brought our own
hydrogen to Mars, we end up with a 1:1 mole

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:51.810
ratio of oxygen and methane, which isn’t
enough to burn our methane completely.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:57.190
So, if we are going this route, we need a
way to produce additional oxygen.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:01.931
The best way is to use another process which
would benefit from Mars’ carbon dioxide

00:12:01.931 --> 00:12:06.120
rich atmosphere - the Reverse Water Gas Shift
reaction (RWGS).

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:11.870
This looks very similar to the Sabatier process,
and involves the reaction of carbon dioxide

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:17.400
with hydrogen, but instead of producing methane
and water, it produces carbon monoxide and

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:18.400
water.

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:22.930
The water that’s formed in this reaction
can then be electrolysed, and the hydrogen

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:27.670
recycled back into the reactor
Again, when the equations of the processes

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:33.520
are combined, it gives us the 2 : 1 molar
ratio between oxygen and methane that’s

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.640
needed for the propellant mixture.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:39.770
This gives us options if mining water on Mars
proves difficult.

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:45.089
But the additional advantage of this approach
is that these two reactions - Sabatier and

00:12:45.089 --> 00:12:51.470
Reverse Water Gas Shift - can be done in the
same reactor, as demonstrated by Pioneer Astronautics

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:53.310
in 2005 [3].

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:58.190
This combination of these two reactions has
an enormous thermal advantage.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:03.060
Because the Sabatier reaction is exothermic
- it releases heat energy - and the Reverse

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:09.420
Water Gas Shift reaction is endothermic - it
absorbs heat - it leads to an overall reduction

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:15.610
of 37% in heat generation, improving the efficiency
of the whole process [3].

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:20.640
But despite how long the Sabatier reaction
has been around, the research into optimising

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:24.230
the process is only a couple of decades old.

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:29.330
For most of its life, the Sabatier reaction
has been used to remove carbon dioxide from

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:34.400
hydrogen in the production of ammonia, as
it damages the catalysts used in the process

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:40.070
of making ammonia, but in the last twenty
years, scientists have begun to realise its

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:44.450
potential in helping mitigate the effects
of climate change.

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:49.510
Instead of letting the carbon dioxide formed
from burning fossil fuels into the atmosphere,

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:55.070
what if we could collect it, and turn it back
into fuel using the Sabatier process [7]?

00:13:55.070 --> 00:14:01.029
This would require a method of not only efficiently
capturing carbon dioxide from the air, but

00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:03.820
a method of creating hydrogen.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:08.740
These processes are currently expensive, but
work is being done to reduce the cost in both

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:10.600
energy and money.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:16.180
This could create a realistic economy for
carbon capture to help mitigate climate change,

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:20.550
but we are a long way off from it being cheap
enough.

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:25.590
If the technology advances, it could offer
a method of storing renewable energy over

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:29.160
extremely long periods of time as methane.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:34.510
There are already a handful of Power to Gas
plants and projects in operation, like the

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:39.970
Audi e-gas plant in Germany [3], but it has
not yet reached wide-scale use.

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:45.190
In December of last year, Elon Musk tweeted
that SpaceX is funding programmes into carbon

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:50.210
capture and storage, adding that the research
would also be important for SpaceX’s Mars

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:51.210
missions.

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:56.430
It’s not yet clear exactly what they are
developing, but it will likely be very similar

00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:03.310
to the Power to Gas projects already in development,
based on the century-old Sabatier reaction.

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:08.480
People often criticise NASA and other space
initiatives as a waste of time, why work on

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:13.890
scientific research to help us live in space
or on other planets when we have so many issues

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:18.850
that need research here on earth, but it’s
clear for anyone paying attention.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:23.779
Working on difficult problems to make Mars
habitable, will directly lead to helping solve

00:15:23.779 --> 00:15:27.490
the greatest problem facing Earth today.

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:29.810
This problem needs all hands on deck.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:34.620
We need as many talented engineers and scientists
working on climate change as possible.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.430
That’s part of the mission of this channel,
